/**
\page py_style Style Guide: Python

\section overview Overview

Python is SDR's second most common language. It is used primarily for developer tools
and situations where development speed is the most important factor over runtime or
bespoke UI.

Our Python coding style is nowhere near as unique as our C style. Most of our Python
conventions are consistent with the language's usual style since Python devs follow
a much more consistent style across projects.

Much of this guide was written by [Journey Hancock (2026)](https://github.com/journeyhancock)
as a guide for SDECv2. Journey was the SDEC project lead for over a year and helped shape the 
organization's Python practices.

\subsection contact Contact

The authoritative source for style decisions in Sun Devil Rocketry's Python code is the
project lead for each project, and then the SDEC project lead, which is currently
[Eli Sells](https://github.com/etsells).

\section golden_rule SDR Style Guide Golden Rule

All of these rules are general guidance to ensure uniformity and readability in our
code. That goal of making our code readable is the most important thing: if you
need to violate a rule in order to make your code readable, then please do that instead
of conforming blindly to these standards. However, in most scenarios, these rules
should work fine.

\section license Licensing

Every source file must begin with the SPDX license header for the project's chosen
license. We use the BSD-3-Clause by default at Sun Devil Rocketry:

\verbatim
# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
# Copyright (c) 2025 Sun Devil Rocketry
\endverbatim

This applies to all `.py` files, including `__init__.py`.

\section naming Naming Conventions

| Element | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Classes | `PascalCase` | `BaseController`, `SerialObj` |
| Functions / Methods | `snake_case` | `open_comport`, `flash_extract` |
| Variables | `snake_case` | `preset_data`, `sensor_frame_names` |
| Constants | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `FLASH_SIZE` |
| Private methods | Leading underscore | `_parse_preset`, `_compute_frames` |
| Module-level files | `snake_case` | `serial_controller.py`, `create_configs.py` |
| Factory files | `create_<subject>.py` | `create_controllers.py`, `create_configs.py` |

\section imports Imports

Imports follow this order, separated by a blank line between each group:

1. Standard library (`builtins`, `json`, `os`, `struct`, `time`)
2. Third-party packages (`serial`, `pandas`)
3. Internal relative imports (`.module`)
4. Cross-package absolute imports (`SDECv2.BaseController`, `SDECv2.SerialController`)
5. `typing` imports (`List`, `Callable`, `Generator`)

\verbatim
import json
import os
import struct

import pandas as pd
import serial

from .preset_config import PresetConfig, ConfigEntry
from .preset_data import PresetData

from SDECv2.SerialController import SerialObj

from typing import List
\endverbatim

Wildcard imports (`from module import *`) are strongly discouraged, and the SDECv2 project even 
disallows them outright. 

These can cause the python interpreter in VSCode to incorrectly show import errors and are generally
considered bad practice to use.

\section type_annotations Type Annotations

Type annotations are strongly encouraged and are required on many of our projects.

Use `|` union syntax (Python 3.10+) for optional or multi-type parameters.

\verbatim
def poll(
    self,
    serial_connection: SerialObj,
    timeout: int | None = None,
    count: int | None = None
) -> Generator[float | int, None, None]:
\endverbatim

Use `typing.List`, `typing.Callable`, and `typing.Generator` for complex generic types. 
For forward references to the enclosing class, use a string literal:

\verbatim
@classmethod
def from_file(cls, path: str) -> "Parser":
\endverbatim

\section docstrings Docstrings

It is best practice to give every class and public method a docstring. Use the following format:

\verbatim
def method(self, arg: Type) -> ReturnType:
    """
    One-sentence summary of what this method does.

    Args:
        arg (Type): Description of the argument.

    Returns:
        ReturnType: Description of the return value.

    Raises:
        ExceptionType: When and why this is raised.
    """
\endverbatim

- The `Raises` section is included only when the method explicitly raises exceptions.
- Private methods (prefixed with `_`) follow the same docstring format.
- Inline comments are used for non-obvious protocol details, especially serial byte sequences.

\section classes Classes

Classes should use `PascalCase` and include a two-line class-level docstring: one sentence describing 
the class purpose, and one sentence describing what it provides.

\verbatim
class Parser:
    """
    Parses and manages preset configurations and data for the Flight Computer.
    Provides methods to load, verify, upload, and manipulate presets.
    """
\endverbatim

`__str__` and `__repr__` should be implemented for all major model classes. 

\verbatim
def __str__(self):
    return (
        "Base Controller:{" + 
        "\n{}".format(self.firmware) +
        "\n{}".format(self.controller) +
        "\n}"
    )
\endverbatim

`__eq__` and `__hash__` should be implemented together whenever objects are compared 
or used in sets/dicts.
*/